<h2 id="how-often-to-audit">How Often to Audit</h2>

<p>The right audit frequency depends on your site's size, how frequently it changes, and how competitive your market is.</p>

<p><strong>Use release checks</strong> after changes to templates, URLs, CMS configuration, consent, analytics or infrastructure. Keep critical monitoring separate from a periodic audit.</p>

<p><strong>Set recurring reviews</strong> from publishing volume, release rate, business consequence and how quickly the team can act. Stable brochure sites and changing marketplaces need different schedules.</p>

<p><strong>After major changes</strong> — regardless of your regular schedule, always audit after a site redesign, CMS migration, domain change, major content restructure, or significant drop in traffic. These events introduce the highest risk of SEO damage.</p>

<p>Between full audits, use monitoring tools to watch for critical issues. Google Search Console email alerts, Ahrefs or Semrush scheduled site audits, and uptime monitoring catch problems in real time so you do not have to wait for the next scheduled audit.</p>

<h2 id="next-steps">Next Steps</h2>

<p>Now that you understand what a website audit is and why it matters, here is how to take action.</p>

<p><strong>Start with page markup.</strong> Paste HTML into our browser-local <a href="/free-website-audit/">free checker</a>. It does not fetch the site, crawl URLs, test SSL, accessibility or speed, or prove Google indexation; use it to triage the supplied HTML.</p>

<p><strong>Use the checklist for a wider review.</strong> Work through the 66-check <a href="/website-audit-checklist/">web checklist</a>, recording URLs, evidence and verification. Pair each check with a tool that actually measures it.</p>

<p><strong>Confirm a professional scope.</strong> AuditWeb services start at USD 297. Confirm the URL set, data access, samples, report, implementation support and meeting requirements in the proposal.</p>

<p>Whichever path you choose, assign an owner and verification check to accepted findings. Measurement after implementation is what distinguishes a resolved issue from a recommendation.</p>

